  "section_notes": "The final text gives both companies something concrete: Northwind's Hearth 10 can keep selling above the 5% test limit under a corrective-action plan, and the shut-off rules are delayed (24-month new-unit start, six-year retrofit). Halcyon's Ember 8 avoids a mandatory recall at 9.9/100k because the 8/100k threshold triggers only review and corrective action, with regulators able to order a recall. S1 is the only section that is strong on its own terms, requiring 24-hour reporting of all thermal incidents."
